# 1. Introduction
 Introduction In recent years Business Process Analysis has gained considerable attention in organisations due to the necessity of maintaining current practices while also seeking ways to be competitive and to create value for stakeholders. There is no point in making assumptions of improvements as these may be unwarranted and instead; organisation should critically examine individual stages of a process in order to pinpoint process delays, duplication of work, potential hazards/risks, bottlenecks and the ability for process improvement. A visual, objective image of how things work and a clear indication of how they might be improved prior to making any changes to process will often justify such changes or allow the organization to develop more effectively solutions.



Within the purpose of this Portfolio I propose to conduct this research which explores the practice of process analysis and I have studied the topic with research supported by an article from a process-oriented literature.



I examined a range of techniques utilised within the context of business process analysis and identified areas in which organisation can identify the need for improvements and suggest suitable recommendations in process enhancement.
